        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Natufians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>: mostly Israel and the Palestinian territories, also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Jordan, the Sinai Peninsula and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a>; key sites Shuqba Cave, el-Wad, Ain Mallaha, Hayonim Cave, Hilazon Tachtit, Raqefet Cave, Nahal Ein Gev II, Tell Abu Hureyra, Shubayqa 1</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 15,000–11,500 years before present; radiocarbon dates of c. 12,500–9,500 BCE; Early Natufian c. 12,000–10,800 BCE, Late Natufian c. 10,800–9,500 BCE (in step with the Younger Dryas)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">settled or semi-settled villages before agriculture; the earliest known food storage; the first sickle blades; the oldest known bread-like food; possible early beer-brewing; burials with dogs; the Hilazon Tachtit grave; the Ain Sakhri lovers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated cave and open-air sites (around 90 excavated), stone and bone tools, plant and animal remains, burials and grave goods, radiocarbon dating and ancient DNA studies</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
