    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Natufians lived by hunting and gathering, yet their settlements were larger and more permanent than any before them, some with round stone houses and the first known food stores.</li><li>They cut wild cereals with stone sickles and made a bread-like food thousands of years before farming, and they may be the ancestors of the builders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>’s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements.</li><li>Dorothy Garrod named the culture in 1929 after the Wadi Natuf; ancient DNA shows that later Levantines descend mainly from the Natufians.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At ʿAin Mallaha in Israel, about 12,000 BCE, an elderly person was buried together with a puppy four to five months old.</p></aside>
    </section>

